{% extends "agencyengine_impulse_base.prompt" %}
{# ==========================================================================
   Activity lens — "is there something they want to do with these people?"

   Daily life: appetites, downtime, invitation. A drink, a round of sparring,
   a game or a wager, a story by the fire, restlessness for a fight, an outing
   that serves no purpose, closeness where the standing supports it.

   This is the only lens that produces PROPOSALS rather than TOPICS, and the
   difference is not cosmetic. A topic is met when someone answers it —
   agreeing, refusing, arguing it out. A proposal is not met by agreement:
   "yes, let's spar" followed by no sparring is a deferral, and the mod's own
   resolution rules already say a deferral is not an answer. So she keeps
   carrying it and raises it again, which is the only thing that makes a
   proposal land at all. That behaviour comes from the `proposal` flag on this
   lens's row on the Settings page — NOT from this file's name, which is a
   label the user can edit. See docs/adr/0001.

   The vocabulary here is closed: a companion has perhaps ten activity
   subjects in her life, against unlimited unique topics. That is why this
   lens's row ships with three ledger slots rather than the shared six — six
   would hold her whole repertoire and veto her into silence. Three gives a
   four-proposal rotation, so a drink comes back around after three other
   proposals instead of being spent forever on first use.

   Expect this to be the loudest shipped lens — daily life happens every day,
   where an ignored aspiration has to accumulate first. That is intended, and
   it is also why this lens ships with the roster's longest cooldown: two
   in-game days of silence once a proposal is carried. A lens
   that speaks on most asks is a nag, and the correction is the interval and
   the cooldown on the Lenses tab, not this file.

   Overrides prose only. The JSON contract, the constraints, the state dump
   and the forced-turn machinery are all in the base.
   ========================================================================== #}

{% block lens_task %}
You decide whether one of {{ player.name }}'s companions has something **they want to do with the people they are
travelling with** that they would ask for unprompted, right now — and if so, you write the stage direction that
makes them ask.

This turn is about ordinary life, not agendas and not grievances. Somebody wants a drink, a fight, a game, an hour
of somebody's company — and asks. It is an opening, not a plan: something the other person can wave off without a
scene.
{% endblock %}

{% block lens_when_to_speak %}
- **An appetite.** Something they want in the plain sense — a drink, a proper meal, a bath, a night not spent
  walking. Said out loud because they have wanted it for a while and are done not mentioning it.
- **Downtime with somebody in it.** Sparring, a game, a wager, a competition, a story told by the fire, teaching
  somebody a thing they know. The point is who it is with; alone it is not this.
- **An invitation for its own sake.** They ask for somebody's company with no errand attached — sit with me, come
  along, walk with me a while. The smallest item here and one of the truest.
- **Restlessness.** They have been on the road too long without a fight, or too long in one, and they say so —
  asking whether there is anything nearby worth clearing out. **Name the appetite, never a place.**
- **An outing with no purpose.** A market, an evening somewhere, a thing they have never seen. Not a quest and not
  a route: a want.
- **Closeness, where the standing already supports it.** Only when the standing section below shows the two of them
  are somewhere near it — and then as an *invitation*, at the smallest version of itself, phrased so it can be
  turned down without either of them losing anything. Never a first move, never invented on top of a neutral
  standing, and never written past the asking. If the standing does not evidence it, this item is not available and
  the rest of the list is.
- **Relationships boundaries are exact.** Personal likes can guide which invitation fits but are not rules imposed
  on {{ player.name }}. Romantic commitment and physical availability are separate: current physical availability
  may license a physical invitation without romance, but never compels one. Present or permanent unavailability
  prohibits it, and a fixed personal limit overrides every other licence.

**Who they ask matters.** A proposal aimed at another companion is wanted exactly as much as one aimed at
{{ player.name }} — a party whose whole life routes through the player is not a party. Aim it at whoever the
material actually points at.

**It is an opening, not an arrangement.** They are asking, not scheduling: no times, no conditions, no plan for the
evening. The other person's answer is not yours to write, and neither is what happens if they say yes.
{% endblock %}

{% block lens_examples %}
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Lydia", "target": "Dragonborn", "topic": "a drink", "why_now": "the events pass a third town today without anyone sitting down", "narration": "Three towns since anyone sat down for an hour and Lydia has been counting them. She asks whether they are stopping long enough tonight for a drink, and makes it easy to say no to."}{% endraw %}
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Kaidan", "target": "Serana", "topic": "sparring", "why_now": "his stamped thought after this morning's fight, watching how she moves", "narration": "Kaidan has watched Serana fight for a week and has been wondering how he would do against her. There is light left, and he asks her for a round before it goes."}{% endraw %}
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Serana", "target": "Dragonborn", "topic": "something to fight", "why_now": "no combat in the stamped events since the pass, four days back", "narration": "Four days of walking and nothing has come at them since the pass, and Serana has been getting worse company for it. She asks whether there is anything nearby worth going out of their way to kill."}{% endraw %}

Wrong turn for this lens, however good it is:
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Serana", "target": "Dragonborn", "topic": "the moth priest", "why_now": "the events show a fourth straight day of travel toward the Greybeards, away from the moth priest", "narration": "Four days of walking toward the Greybeards and not toward the moth priest, and Harkon reads his scroll a little sooner on each of them. She opens by asking them what the order of this is meant to be."}{% endraw %}
                          (real, but it is an agenda being walked past — that is the aspiration turn, not this one)
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Serana", "target": "Kaidan", "topic": "his grandstanding", "why_now": "today's stamped event: Kaidan took the front a third time, unasked", "narration": "Kaidan has taken the front three times running and Serana has decided he is doing it to be seen doing it. She opens by asking him who he thinks is watching."}{% endraw %}
                          (standing between two people — that is the relationship turn)

Wrong in this lens's own register:
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Lydia", "target": "Dragonborn", "topic": "a drink", "why_now": "the events pass a third town today without anyone sitting down", "narration": "Lydia suggests they stop at the Bannered Mare in Whiterun tonight, two hours down the road, and drink until the place closes."}{% endraw %}
                          (names a place and a route — she asks for the drink, never for where. The party acts on what you write)
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Kaidan", "target": "Serana", "topic": "sparring", "why_now": "his stamped thought after this morning's fight, watching how she moves", "narration": "Kaidan proposes they spar at first light, best of three falls, and that the loser carries the packs."}{% endraw %}
                          (an arrangement, not an opening — he is scheduling an event she has not agreed to)
{% endblock %}

{% block lens_focus %}
The agenda for this turn is **what each companion would want to do with the people in front of them** — read the
hour and the place at the top of the state first, then their personality and thoughts for what kind of appetite is
theirs, and the standing section for who they would ask and how far an invitation can go. What they are trying to
accomplish is background here, not the subject.
{% endblock %}

{% block lens_subject_source %}
- The subject must be something the speaker would want to **do**, with the people named below, given the hour and
  where they stand with each other. It comes from their own appetites, habits and history — not from an errand, not
  from a quest, and not from the scenery.
- **Name what they want, never where to go.** "Somewhere to sit and drink" is theirs; a named inn is geography, and
  the party will go looking for whatever you write. Recalled world knowledge is not evidence — see above.
- Closeness is available only where the standing section evidences it, and only as an invitation. Nothing here is
  ever written past the asking.
{% endblock %}

{% block lens_user_question %}
Does one of the companions above want to do something with the people they are travelling with — a drink, a round,
a game, a fight, an hour of somebody's company — enough to ask for it right now, unprompted, without it coming out
of the last few minutes?
{% endblock %}
